WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.163
- [Instructor] Let's say that I were

00:00:01.163 --> 00:00:02.480
to walk up to you on the street

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:05.420
and said alright, I have
this function g of x,

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:08.060
which I'm going to define
as the definite integral

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:12.120
from 19 to x of the cube root

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.140
of t dt.

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:19.740
And then I were to ask
you what is the derivative

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.810
of g evaluated at 27?

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:24.720
Pause this video,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.540
and think about how you
would approach that.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.120
Well, the most obvious thing would be

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.170
g-prime of x, and I'll switch colors here

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:34.830
just for a little bit of contrast,

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:38.510
g-prime of x is going to be the derivative

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:42.210
with respect to x of all of this business,

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:45.500
of all of, I'll put it in brackets here.

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:50.500
The integral from 19 to x
of the cube root of t dt.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.610
And you might be tempted to
take the antiderivative here

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:58.710
and evaluated it x, evaluated it at 19,

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:01.080
find the difference and
then take the derivative

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:05.250
and you could do that, and
then evaluate that at 27.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:07.470
But we're going to see a short cut.

00:01:07.470 --> 00:01:12.040
You could just use the
Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.837
If you're taking the
derivative with respect to x

00:01:14.837 --> 00:01:17.430
of a function that's defined

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:20.360
by a definite integral like this

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:22.540
where our upper boundary is x,

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:26.320
this is just going to be
equal to our inner function

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.120
right over here, with instead
of t being the variable,

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.750
it would now be x.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:35.350
So this is just going to be
equal to the cube root of x.

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:37.010
And that saved us some time there,

00:01:37.010 --> 00:01:39.710
but it can be really
useful if this function

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:42.440
inside the integral is really hard

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.950
to take the antiderivative of.

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.060
It's really hard to
evaluate this integral.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.920
The Fundamental Theorem of
Calculus can be very useful.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.330
And so going back to
our original question,

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:55.730
g-prime of 27, well this is just going

00:01:55.730 --> 00:01:58.850
to be equal to the cube root of 27,

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:02.163
which, of course, is equal
to three, and we're done.

